   |\nL194: cite81†assets | cite81†assets |    |\nL195: cite82†benches | cite82†benches |    |\nL196: cite83†docs | cite83†docs |    |\nL197: cite84†src | cite84†src |    |\nL198: cite85†tests | cite85†tests |    |\nL199: cite86†.gitignore | cite86†.gitignore |    |\nL200: cite87†CHANGELOG.md | cite87†CHANGELOG.md |    |\nL201: cite88†Cargo.toml | cite88†Cargo.toml |    |\nL202: cite89†LICENSE-APACHE | cite89†LICENSE-APACHE |    |\nL203: cite90†LICENSE-MIT | cite90†LICENSE-MIT |    |\nL204: cite91†README.md | cite91†README.md |    |\nL205: cite92†SECURITY.md | cite92†SECURITY.md |    |\nL206: [Button: View all files]\nL207: ## Repository files navigation\nL208: \nL209:   *   * cite93†README L210:   * cite93†Apache-2.0 license L211:   * cite93†MIT license L212:   * cite93†Security L213: \nL214: More items\nL215: # Efficient Sumcheck\nL216: \nL217: A high-performance sumcheck library with cite94†correctness-fuzzing against a verified oracle.\nL218: \nL219:   * Efficient — transparent SIMD acceleration (8-wide AVX-512, 2-wide NEON)\nL220:   * Streaming-capable — optional sublinear memory via sequential evaluation\nL221:   * Complete — built-in Fiat-Shamir, partial execution, per-round hooks\nL222: \nL223: Built using cite95†arkworks . Compatible with any ecosystem — see cite96†`docs/compatibility.md` .\nL224: \nL225: ## Quick Start\nL226: ### Multilinear Sumcheck\nL227: \nL228: Proves $H = \\sum_{x \\in \\lbrace 0,1 \\rbrace^v} p(x)$ where $p$ is a multilinear polynomial.\nL229: \nL230:     use effsc::{noop_hook, runner::sumcheck};\nL231:     use effsc::provers::multilinear::MultilinearProver;\nL232: \nL233:     let mut prover = MultilinearProver::new(evals);\nL234:     let proof = sumcheck(\nL235:         &mut prover,\nL236:         num_vars,\nL237:         &mut transcript,\nL238:         noop_hook,\nL239:     );\nL240: ### Inner Product Sumcheck\nL241: \nL242: Proves $H = \\sum_{x \\in \\lbrace 0,1 \\rbrace^v} f(x) \\cdot g(x)$ for two multilinear polynomials. Degree-2 round polynomials.\nL243: \nL244:     use effsc::{noop_hook, runner::sumcheck};\nL245:     use effsc::provers::inner_product::InnerProductProver;\nL246: \nL247:     let mut prover = InnerProductProver::new(a, b);\nL248:     let proof = sumcheck(\nL249:         &mut prover,\nL250:         num_vars,\nL251:         &mut transcript,\nL252:         noop_hook,\nL253:     );\nL254: ### Coefficient Sumcheck\nL255: Proves $H = \\sum_{x \\in \\lbrace 0,1 \\rbrace^v} p(x)$ where $\\deg_{x_i}(p) \\leq d$. The user implements `RoundPolyEvaluator` to define per-pair round polynomial contributions; the library handles iteration, parallelism, and reductions.\nL256: \nL257:     use effsc::{noop_hook, runner::sumcheck};\nL258:     use effsc::provers::coefficient::CoefficientProver;\nL259: \nL260:     let mut prover = CoefficientProver::new(\nL261:         &evaluator,\nL262:         tablewise,\nL263:         pairwise,\nL264:     );\nL265:     let proof = sumcheck(\nL266:         &mut prover,\nL267:         num_rounds,\nL268:         &mut transcript,\nL269:         noop_hook,\nL270:     );\nL271: ### Verification\nL272: One verifier for any degree $d$. Returns `SumcheckResult { challenges, final_claim }` — ⚠️ the caller is responsible for the oracle check (cite97†Thaler Remark 4.2†people.cs.georgetown.edu ).\nL273: \nL274:     use effsc::{noop_hook_verify, verifier::sumcheck_verify};\nL275: \nL276:     let result = sumcheck_verify(\nL277:         claimed_sum,\nL278:         degree,\nL279:         num_rounds,\nL280:         &mut transcript,\nL281:         noop_hook_verify,\nL282:     )?;\nL283: \nL284:     // Standalone: compare against the prover's claimed final value.\nL285:     assert_eq!(result.final_claim, proof.final_value);\nL286: \nL287:     // Composed (WHIR, GKR): pass final_claim to the next layer.\nL288:     next_layer_claim = result.final_claim;\nL289: ## Variable Ordering\nL290: \nL291: Each prover comes in two variants:\nL292: \nL293:   * MSB (half-split) — optimal memory layout in most cases. Used by WHIR and WARP.\nL294:   * LSB (pair-split) — optimal for streaming applications where evaluations arrive sequentially.\nL295: MSB  | LSB\nL296: --- | ---\nL297: `MultilinearProver`  | `MultilinearProverLSB`\nL298: `InnerProductProver`  | `InnerProductProverLSB`\nL299: `CoefficientProver`  | `CoefficientProverLSB`\nL300: `EqFactoredProver`  | —\nL301: `GkrProver`  | —\nL302: \nL303: See cite98†`docs/design.md` for details.\nL304: ## Partial Execution and Hooks\nL305: \nL306: The `sumcheck()` runner supports partial execution (`num_rounds < v`) and per-round hooks for composed protocols:\nL307: \nL308:     // WHIR: partial sumcheck with proof-of-work grinding\nL309:     let proof = sumcheck(\nL310:         &mut prover,\nL311:         folding_factor,  // num_rounds < v\nL312:         &mut transcript,\nL313:         |_, t| round_pow.prove(t),  // per-round hook\nL314:     );\nL315: ## SIMD Acceleration\nL316: \nL317: All provers transparently auto-dispatch to SIMD backends. Supported fields:\nL318: \nL319:   * [Input] Goldilocks ($p = 2^{64} - 2^{32} + 1$) and degree-2/3 extensions\nL320:   * [Input] M31 ($p = 2^{31} - 1$) and extensions\nL321:   * [Input] BabyBear ($p = 2^{31} - 2^{27} + 1$) and extensions\nL322:   * [Input] KoalaBear ($p = 2^{31} - 2^{24} + 1$) and extensions\nL323: Backend  | Width  | Platform\nL324: --- | --- | ---\nL325: NEON  | 2-wide  | aarch64 (Apple M-series, Graviton)\nL326: AVX-512 IFMA  | 8-wide  | x86_64 (Sapphire Rapids)\nL327: Scalar for other fields. See cite99†`SECURITY.md` .\nL328: ## Examples\nL329: \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nefficient-sumcheck/docs/design.md at main · compsec-epfl/efficient-sumcheck · GitHub (https://github.com/compsec-epfl/efficient-sumcheck/blob/main/docs/design.md)\nciteturn1view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Cont